hey there my name is Cameron welcome to  3d layer techniques these training  videos are a complement to the 3d camera  technique series so if you haven't  watched that series yet please do and  the next several videos you are going to  learn about 3d layers inside of After  Effects flat 2d motion design can look  great but adding 3d elements can make  motion design awesome so let's dive in  and get started  this is motion science  to make a 2d layer 3d the process is  simple go to the timeline and with the  selection tool click here  using the default classic render a 3d  layer remains flat but properties for Z  position anchor point scale orientation  and rotation all become active by  default 3d layers of AZ position value  of zero meaning that they stay centered  in the scene access modes help to orient  the 3d layer position and rotation and  after effects there are three axis modes  local aligns the x y and z axes to the  3d layer world mode aligns the axes to  the composition so no matter the changes  to a layers rotation the axes are always  relative to the composition and View  mode aligns the axes to the selected  view by placing a 3d camera in the scene  and moving around the axes will always  orient towards the camera view to  position or rotate the 3d layer on its  axes use the selection and rotation  tools found in the tools panel the  shortcut for selection is V and the  shortcut for rotation is W  3d layers can also be positioned or  rotated by going to the timeline panel  and directly inputting numbers for the  property values or by hovering over the  property clicking and dragging by  holding down the shift key while  dragging property values can change more  quickly to higher or lower values a pro  tip that is sometimes useful to control  orientation of 3d layers is to use the  auto orient function  go to layer transform Auto Orient and  choose orient along path when a layer  needs to follow a motion path or orient  towards camera when a layer needs to  always face the camera  material options specify how 3d layers  interact with lights and shadows to  access material options quickly with the  3d layer selected tap a a on the  keyboard the first material option casts  shadows enables a 3d layer to cast  shadows on other 3d layers the three  modes are off on and only only turns the  layer visibility off but the layer still  casts the shadow light transmission is  the amount of color from a 3d layer that  is cast as a shadow no light  transmission produces a dark black  shadow while a value of 100% light  transmission casts 100% of the layer  color onto the shadow accepts shadows  turns on the layers ability to receive  shadows from another 3d layer this  option can also be set to OFF on or only  only will act as a shadow catcher and  reveal the accepted shadow on the  transparent layer accepts lights enables  a 3d layer to accept lights within the  scene ambient is the non directional  reflectivity of the layer and is like  having a layer equally lit from all  directions diffuse is the Omni  directional reflectivity of the layer  the further away the layer is from a  light source the more darkness will  appear in the layer  specular intensity is the directional  reflectivity of the layer and how shiny  the layer appears specular shininess is  the size of the specular highlight and  glossiness of the reflection glossiness  can range from blurry to mere light and  metal is the contribution of the layer  color to the color of the specular  highlight a value of 100% metal means  that the highlight color is the color of  the layer  unlike 2d layers 3d layers interact with  shadows and lights After Effects uses  four types of lights that are similar to  real-world lights add a new light to the  composition by going to layer new light  or use the shortcut shift command Y  parallel lights emit directional light  from a very long distance  spotlights emit from a cone shape point  lights emit in all directions and  ambient lights emit with no light source  or shadow but add to the overall  luminance of a scene each of the four  light types have options intensity is  the brightness of the light intensity  can be both positive and negative  meaning it can add to or take away light  from a scene color determines the light  color a pro tip is to stay away from  100% white even slightly off white  lights can make a composition more  visually appealing  fall-off is how the lights intensity  lessens over a distance there are three  types the first none represents no  decrease in light intensity no matter  the distance that the light travels the  second smooth represents a linear  fall-off starting at the radius and  extending by the fall-off distance and  the third inverse-square clamped is a  physically accurate fall-off  starting at the radius and decreasing  proportionally the further away the  light gets radius is the area of  fall-off fall-off distance is the  distance the light falls from a light  source cast shadows is whether or not  the light casts a shadow on 3d layers  shadow darkness is how dark the shadow  appears point and spotlights have an  additional option for shadow softness  known as shadow diffusion and finally  spotlights add cone angle and feather  cone angle is the width size of the cone  opening and feather is the edge softness  of that opening  a pro tip is to adjust shadowmap  resolution for scenes using lights that  take too long to render or don't look  quality enough in the final render to  adjust shadow map resolution go to  composition composition settings 3d  render options from here a preset can be  selected to increase or decrease the  shadow map resolution  layer stacking within the timeline can  affect how 2d and 3d layers interact  with each other and how a final  composition will look normally one 3d  layer that is lit and stacked in front  of another 3d layer will cast a shadow  on the underneath layer but when a 2d  layer is inserted into the layer stack a  3d layer will not cast a shadow on any  other 3d layer that is on the other side  of the 2d layer in the stacking order  this also applies to 3d layer  intersections a pro tip is to place an  adjustment layer between the 3d layers  where the unwanted shadow or  intersection is seen the adjustment  layer is 2d but remains invisible and  the unwanted shadow or intersection will  disappear other types of layers can  break unwanted shadows and intersections  as well they are 3d layers with applied  layer styles 3d pre comps with applied  effects closed masks or track mats or 3d  pre comps without collapse  transformations  new to After Effects CC 2017 is the  cinema 4d rendering engine by using the  cinema 4d renderer a three layer inside  of After Effects can be bent beveled and  extruded to enable this renderer press  command K on the keyboard to open up  composition settings click on the 3d  renderer tab from here select cinema 4d  a list of enabled features and  limitations will appear please take the  time to read through them click on the  options tab the new quality slider is  meant to make it easy and take out the  guesswork and setting ray threshold  great depth reflection depth shadow  depth anti-aliasing and reflectance all  by just dragging left or right choose a  quality setting that has a good render  speed and acceptable image quality  before rendering always slide the  quality slider to the right to produce  the best image results for a final  render now to bend a 3d layer first  ensure that the selected layer is 3d in  the timeline then open up the geometry  options by twirling down this arrow note  that layer bending only works on  imported footage solid layers and pre  comps since layer bending does not work  on native text the solution is to pre  compose the text first  adjust the curvature between zero and  100% geometry segments determine how  many segments the curved layer is broken  into the default segment number is 4  double the number of segments until a  desired smoothness level is achieved  with the cinema 4d renderer 3d text and  shape layers can be beveled and extruded  Z depth is added by increasing extrusion  increase bevel and bevel hole depth to  give outer and inner edge depth slide  between values of 1 and 10 or directly  input a number above 10 for larger  bevels there are 4 bevel styles built-in  none angular concave and convex  additional material options for  reflections also become available when  using the cinema 4d renderer when  working with reflections footage and  nested pre comps can be used to  aspherical e mapped environment layers  to make interesting reflections to make  an environment layer choose layer  environment layer or right click on the  layer in the timeline the layer will  switch from 2d to 3d and a small globe  icon will appear next to its name  properties for orientation rotation and  transparency are all animatable appears  and reflections indicates if the current  3d layer appears in other layers  reflections reflection intensity  controls how much other reflective 3d  layers appear on the 3d layer reflection  sharpness controls the sharpness or  blurriness of reflections reflection  roll-off controls the amount of fernell  effect which is the intensity of  reflections at certain angles a pro tip  is to slightly vary materials on  different surfaces so that each surface  is not exactly the same this can make  compositions more visually appealing to  do this technique open up the shape  properties located next to contents  click on the arrow for add the different  side options for front bevel side and  back are available here on text layers  these options can be found in the  properties under the animate arrow  in the Irate race 3d renderer a 3d layer  has the same geometry options as found  in the cinema 4d renderer bending  beveling and extrusions  but the raytrace renderer produces a  slightly different look to enable this  renderer press command K on the keyboard  to open up composition settings click on  the 3d renderer tab from here select  raytrace 3d a list of features and  limitations similar to the cinema 4d  renderer will appear take the time to  read through these as well a pro tip is  to use the cinema 4d renderer whenever  possible it is faster than the rate race  renderer a second pro tip to maximize  workflow while using the raytrace  renderer is to set the composition panel  preview mode to direct this setting is  the equivalent of setting a rate raise  quality to 1 in composition settings but  be aware of limitations with shadows and  reflections material options for the  raytrace render are the same as the  cinema 4d render with 2 added options  transparency and environment layer  options transparency controls the  materials transparency surfaces can be  fully transparent the still show  reflections and specular highlights like  scene and glass transparency roll off  controls the amount of transparency  relative to the viewing angle on a  transparent surface and index of  refraction controls how light bends and  distorts through 3d layers with a value  of 1 equaling no distortion and a value  of 5 equalling solid glass or water when  using environment layers with the  raytrace renderer an option for appears  and reflections will be enabled this  controls how the environment map is used  in the scene set to on the environment  appears both in reflective objects and  as a backdrop  set to only the environment appears only  in reflective objects and set to OFF the  environment appears only as a backdrop  to the scene a pro tip is to use the  effect stylize cz repet I'll whenever  seams are visible in the reflection this  effect helps to blend seams  cinah where is a powerful tool it  enables 3d layers to have even more  advanced shapes bevels and textures  because 3d layers are built inside of  cinema 4d by composited and rendered  inside of After Effects a version of  cinema 4d comes packaged with After  Effects CC with sin aware After Effects  can import any pre-built cinema 4d  project or export any composition  directly to cinema 4d to import a cinema  4d project go to file import and import  the file make a new composition by going  to composition new composition and drag  the cinema 4d file from the project  window into the composition the cinah  where effect is automatically added and  can be found in the effects panel to  edit the cinema 4d file select the file  in the timeline and go to edit edit  original cinema 4d will launch make any  necessary changes inside a cinema 4d and  then save the file in After Effects the  cinema 4d file is automatically updated  to synchronize time lines between cinema  4d and After Effects enable Livelink  render settings control how the image  looks and tells after effects which  cinema 4d renderer to use the camera is  selected under project settings choose  from baked in cameras in the cinema 4d  file  a new centered comp camera in After  Effects for an existing After Effects  camera already in the timeline cinema 40  layers Wynn checked allow four layers to  be on or off  within the cinema 45 this means a cinema  4d file could turn on or off 3d elements  internally by duplicating the cinema 4d  file and setting each of these layers to  show individual elements compositing  becomes easier because of the ability to  stack layers  multipass image layers enables specific  compositing layers to be extracted into  After Effects and lastly commands  extracts cinema 4d scene data directly  into After Effects  this can include cameras lights and  position data from external compositing  tags in this series we explored how to  enable and control 3d layers how to work  with lights and shadows how to utilize  material options and how to take  advantage of the advanced renderers and  settings now that you know the  techniques to master 3d layers inside of  After Effects it's time to put that  knowledge into practice download the  free After Effects project file below  and explore how I use different 3d layer  techniques to build it practice makes  perfect and the more you practice the  better you will become as a motion  designer I would love to hear your  thoughts about this training series and  what you would like to see in future  series so please leave a comment below  thanks for taking the time to watch and  until next time I'm Cameron and this is  motion science  you
 

